\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces Eight different NLDR representations of a human PBMC CITE-seq dataset (Hao et al. (\citeproc {ref-hao2021}{2021})). Different techniques and different hyper-parameter choices are used (\emph  {(a) UMAP (n\_neighbors = 15, min\_dist = 0.1), (b) UMAP (n\_neighbors = 54, min\_dist = 0.5), (c) PaCMAP (n\_neighbors = 51, init = random, MN\_ratio = 0.3, FP\_ratio = 2), (d) tSNE (perplexity = 30), (e) tSNE (perplexity = 84), (f) PHATE (knn = 5), (g) TriMAP (n\_inliers = 12, n\_outliers = 4, n\_random = 3), (h) PaCMAP (n\_neighbors = 10, init = random, MN\_ratio = 0.5, FP\_ratio = 2)}). Researchers may have seen any of these in their analysis of this data, depending on their choice of method, or typical hyper-parameter choice. Would they make different decisions downstream in the analysis depending on which version seen? Which is the most accurate representation of the structure in high dimensions?}}{1}{figure.caption.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig-NLDR-variety-intro}{{1.1}{1}{Eight different NLDR representations of a human PBMC CITE-seq dataset (Hao et al. (\citeproc {ref-hao2021}{2021})). Different techniques and different hyper-parameter choices are used (\emph {(a) UMAP (n\_neighbors = 15, min\_dist = 0.1), (b) UMAP (n\_neighbors = 54, min\_dist = 0.5), (c) PaCMAP (n\_neighbors = 51, init = random, MN\_ratio = 0.3, FP\_ratio = 2), (d) tSNE (perplexity = 30), (e) tSNE (perplexity = 84), (f) PHATE (knn = 5), (g) TriMAP (n\_inliers = 12, n\_outliers = 4, n\_random = 3), (h) PaCMAP (n\_neighbors = 10, init = random, MN\_ratio = 0.5, FP\_ratio = 2)}). Researchers may have seen any of these in their analysis of this data, depending on their choice of method, or typical hyper-parameter choice. Would they make different decisions downstream in the analysis depending on which version seen? Which is the most accurate representation of the structure in high dimensions?}{figure.caption.6}{}}
