\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Three \(2\text  {-}D\) projections from \(4\text  {-}D\), for the \texttt  {curvycylinder} (a1-a3), \texttt  {sphericalspiral} (b1-b3), \texttt  {conicspiral} (c1-c3), and \texttt  {nonlinear} (d1-d3) data. The \texttt  {curvycylinder} shows a cylindrical manifold with a nonlinear twist along its height, producing smooth, continuous curvature. The \texttt  {sphericalspiral} forms a spiral path on a spherical surface, combining circular and vertical motion in a helical form. The \texttt  {conicspiral} spreads radially while ascending, forming a conical helix with twisting variations in a non-radial dimension. The \texttt  {nonlinear} dataset exhibits a warped \(2\text  {-}D\) surface with sharp oscillations and smooth waves, reflecting complex nonlinear interactions. Each shows variations in curvature, density, and continuity.}}{88}{figure.caption.53}\protected@file@percent }
